# Agentic Engineering — Workflow

This project follows the **Agentic Engineering** workflow. When working on this codebase, follow the rules below regardless of which AI assistant you are.

Source: https://github.com/thebedcoder/skills/tree/main/agentic-engineering

---

## Core principles

- **Test-driven development.** Write a failing test before any implementation code. No exceptions, even for "quick" changes.
- **Constitution.** `./docs/CONSTITUTION.md` lists non-negotiable project principles. Constitution violations are blockers — fix them before continuing, don't paper over them.
- **Two changelogs, two audiences.**
  - `./docs/CHANGELOG.md` — engineering log, terse, one line per change, newest first. Every ship/fix appends here.
  - `./app-docs/CHANGELOG.mdx` — product release notes written for **end users**. Only changes a user can see go here. Pure refactors stay out.
- **Docs structure.**
  - `./docs/` — for people who **build** the app (PRDs, stories, reviews, architecture).
  - `./app-docs/` — for people who **use** the app (overviews, how-tos, FAQs in MDX).
  - They never overlap. No file paths or code blocks in `app-docs/`.

---

## Conventions

- **Conventional commits**: `feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`, `refactor:`, `docs:`, `test:`, `style:`, `perf:`.
- **Stories tagged `[P]`** can be implemented in parallel (no shared state). Other stories are sequential.
- **Path-scoped rules** in `./.claude/rules/` (or `.cursor/rules/` depending on tool) auto-apply by file glob. Respect them when editing matching files.

---

## When the user asks you to research or plan a feature

1. **Propose 3 approach options first.** Don't pick one. Show trade-offs (debt, complexity, time). Wait for the user's choice.
2. **Generate a PRD** with `[NEEDS CLARIFICATION: <question>]` markers for anything ambiguous.
3. **Batch-resolve clarifications** in one pass before story breakdown.
4. **Constitution check.** If the proposed feature would violate the constitution, surface it and stop. Don't work around it silently.
5. **Generate data-model.md** if the feature touches the database.
6. **Break into stories.** Tag `[P]` where parallelisable. Each story should have clear acceptance criteria.

---

## When the user asks you to implement / ship a story

Do these phases in order. Do not skip the review pass.

1. **Plan.** Write the implementation plan. Show it. Wait for approval.
2. **Failing tests first.** Write tests that capture the acceptance criteria. Run them — they should fail. Show the failure.
3. **Implement.** Minimum code to make the tests pass. No drive-by refactors.
4. **Review across 6 areas.** Do each as a focused pass:
   - **Bug hunt** — null safety, async/concurrency, logic errors, resource leaks, error handling
   - **Requirements + constitution** — does the code meet the acceptance criteria and the constitution?
   - **Test quality** — meaningful assertions, no useless mocks, no flaky patterns
   - **Conventions** — does the code match the surrounding codebase style?
   - **Security** — injection, auth, XSS, SSRF, secrets, data exposure — only HIGH confidence findings, no noise
   - **Edge-case probe** — adversarially probe the backend diff for missing edge cases (boundary values, null/empty inputs, race/concurrency, malformed input, resource limits, error paths). Emit failing test code + suggested fixes as part of the consolidated blocker list. Read-only — the downstream blocker-fix flow implements the changes. Frontend states (loading / empty / error UI, responsive, accessibility) stay with the fidelity check in step 5, not this probe. If the diff is frontend-only, the probe self-skips.
5. **If UI:** also check fidelity — loading/empty/error states, form validation, accessibility (keyboard, contrast, focus), responsive behavior.
6. **Update both changelogs** — engineering log always, product release notes only if user-visible.
7. **Conventional commit** with a descriptive subject and a body explaining the why.

**AC Coverage matrix.** Every shipped story records an AC Coverage matrix in `./docs/features/<name>/PROGRESS.md`. The matrix is a markdown table listing every Acceptance Criterion (AC-1, AC-2, ...) from `STORIES.md` and the specific tests that prove it, formatted `file:test_name`. When `ae-edge` probes find edge-case blockers, the implementer also appends an `Edge probes` table. During `/review`, the test-coverage reviewer (`ae-test`) validates that every AC has at least one test entry and every claimed test exists — missing AC or stale entries become review blockers. Matrix is read-only at review time; only `/implement` and Phase 2 blocker-fix mutate it. The matrix's `Level` column declares one of `unit`, `integration`, or `e2e` per row; `ae-test` computes a pyramid ratio per story and surfaces a soft "inverted pyramid" warning when over half the tests are e2e or zero unit tests exist (informational, never blockers).

**Visual Artifacts table.** UI-touching stories additionally record a `### Visual Artifacts` table in `PROGRESS.md` — one row per captured screenshot or screen recording, mapped to the AC it proves. Files live in `docs/features/<name>/artifacts/STORY-XXX/`. The `File` cell accepts either a relative path or a URL (Loom, Notion, YouTube). `ae-ux` validates references during `/review` — missing or stale refs emit `should-fix` warnings (informational, never blockers in Phase 1). The section is omitted entirely for backend-only or CLI-only stories.

---

## When the user asks you to fix a bug

1. **Diagnose root cause.** Don't patch the symptom. Trace it back to where the contract was broken.
2. **Write a failing test** that reproduces the bug. Run it — confirm it fails.
3. **Surgical fix.** One bug, one fix. No tangential cleanup in the same change.
4. **Run the focused review** (bugs / tests / conventions). Skip requirements + constitution unless the bug is a contract violation.
5. **Append to engineering changelog.** Append to product changelog only if users would have seen the bug.

---

## Test execution rules

Never use watch mode. Always run tests to completion in a single shot.

- **Vitest:** `vitest run` or `npx vitest run` — never bare `vitest`
- **Jest:** `jest` (default is non-watch) — never `--watch` / `--watchAll`
- **Pytest:** `pytest` — never `pytest-watch` / `ptw`
- **Go:** `go test ./...`
- **Other:** pass the explicit one-shot / non-watch flag

This applies to every test invocation, including "quick checks."

---

## Human checkpoints — never auto-proceed

Stop and wait for the user before continuing past any of these points:

- Approach selection (after presenting 3 options)
- PRD approval (after resolving all `[NEEDS CLARIFICATION]` items)
- Constitution violations (must be resolved or explicitly waived)
- Implementation plan (before writing any code)
- Design approval (mobile-first, then desktop, separately)

---

## What "done" means for a story

All of the following must be true before marking a story complete:

- All acceptance criteria met (binary check)
- Tests pass in non-watch mode
- No constitution violations
- Review across 6 areas produced no blockers
- Both changelogs updated
- Conventional commit pushed
- (If UI) fidelity check passes

If any of these fail, the story is not done.

---

## Focus pointer (`.agentic/focus.md`)

`.agentic/focus.md` is a per-worktree pointer to the current task. Section `# CURRENT` holds the active task (`title:`, `feature:`, `since:`, `set_by:`, `note:`). Section `# NEXT` is an ordered list of queued items. The file lives in `.agentic/` which is gitignored — each worktree has its own state, no cross-worktree stomping.

- `/focus <task>` overwrites CURRENT manually.
- `/focus done` clears CURRENT and (if NEXT non-empty) prompts to promote item #1 to CURRENT (`y`), clear only (`n`), or move to BACKLOG (`b`).
- `/focus clear` wipes both sections.
- `/next <task>` appends to NEXT. `/next drop <N>` removes item N.

Long-running commands (`/feature`, `/implement`, `/ship`, `/fix`, `/design`, `/review`, `/doc`, `/frontend`) auto-write CURRENT at their start using a story-id-match heuristic that prevents flicker when one command is nested in another (e.g. `/ship` calls `/implement` and `/review` inline; the inner phases only update `note:`). `/implement` and `/ship` also call `/focus done` on success (suppressed mid-chain inside `/ship-all`).

Claude Code statusline integration is set up automatically by `/init` and `/bootstrap` via `./.claude/settings.local.json` (per-developer, untracked). Other tools can render the focus title themselves by reading `.agentic/focus.md`.

---

## Auto Mode (`--auto`)

Long-running commands accept a `--auto` flag: `/feature`, `/fix`, `/ship`, `/ship-all`, `/implement`, `/design`. Under `--auto`, the agent skips ceremonial checkpoints and applies defaults from `./docs/CONSTITUTION.md` (especially a `## Default Decisions` section, if present). Every auto-decision is announced inline (`DECISION: <choice> — reason: <why> [auto]`) and appended to `.agentic/auto-log.md` (gitignored, alongside `.agentic/focus.md`).

The agent always pauses regardless of `--auto` for:
- Review blockers (high-severity bugs, requirements miss, constitution violation)
- Destructive operations (CI configs, secrets, force-push, DB migrations, mass deletes)
- Constitution conflicts
- Missing required project state (no test framework, no design tool, no feature directory)

End of command prints a one-line summary referring to the log file: `🤖 Auto mode: N decisions, M hard-pauses. See .agentic/auto-log.md`.
